NAME
    date - print the wall-clock time

SYNOPSIS
    date [-u] [+FORMAT]

DESCRIPTION
    Reads the kernel wall clock and writes one formatted line.

    With no FORMAT the output is ISO 8601 with an explicit offset, so a line is
    unambiguous without knowing which zone the machine runs in:

        2026-07-15T08:00:00-0400

    The zone comes from the TZ value the launcher supplied, which is a POSIX
    zone string such as UTC0 or EST5EDT,M3.2.0,M11.1.0. There is no timezone
    database, so a name like America/New_York is refused rather than looked up,
    and historical transitions before the zone's current rules are not
    represented. -u reports UTC regardless of TZ.

    The zone is the one the session supplies. Set it by writing one POSIX zone
    string to /config/timezone and rebooting:

        printf 'CET-1CEST,M3.5.0,M10.5.0/3' > /config/timezone

    The session reads that file once when it starts, so an edit takes effect at
    the next boot rather than changing processes that are already running. A
    file that does not parse is reported on the boot diagnostic and the session
    falls back to UTC. With no file the zone is UTC.

    The zone cannot be narrowed per command through spawn: a child's
    capabilities must attenuate its launcher's, and spawn holds no wall-clock
    authority to pass on, so spawn refuses the launch rather than starting date
    without a clock.

    +FORMAT selects the C-locale conversions the runtime supports, including
    %Y %m %d %H %M %S %a %A %b %B %j %Z %z and %%. %Z writes the zone
    abbreviation and %z the numeric offset.

    Setting the clock is not part of this command. The timesync service owns
    correction through the privileged clock-control capability.

EXIT STATUS
    0   the time was written
    2   the invocation or FORMAT was rejected
    1   the wall clock is unconfigured or unavailable

EXAMPLES
    date
    date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ
    date '+%H:%M %Z'
